@section Lists and higher-order functions

@noindent
OCaml's built-in @code{'a list} is a proper homogeneous sequence, written
with semicolons and built the same way Scheme's list is: an empty list,
@code{[]}, and @code{::} (pronounced ``cons''), which prepends one element
to an existing list, exactly like Scheme's @code{cons} onto a list built from
nested pairs terminated by @code{'()}:

@example
# 0 :: [ 1; 2; 3 ];;
- : int list = [0; 1; 2; 3]
# [ 1; 2 ] @@ [ 3; 4 ];;
- : int list = [1; 2; 3; 4]
@end example

@noindent
(@code{@@} is list append; two lists in, one list out, in time proportional
to the length of the first.) A list's type names its element type, so
@code{int list} and @code{string list} are different types, and a list
mixing @code{int} and @code{string} elements does not type-check — this is
why a variant, not a mixed list, is this book's way to represent data of
several possible shapes.

@subsubheading Library higher-order functions

@code{List.map} and @code{List.filter} take a function and a list and
produce a new list, translating Scheme's @code{map} and @code{filter}
directly:

@example
# List.map (fun x -> x * x) [ 1; 2; 3; 4 ];;
- : int list = [1; 4; 9; 16]
# List.filter (fun x -> x mod 2 = 0) (List.init 10 (fun i -> i + 1));;
- : int list = [2; 4; 6; 8; 10]
@end example

@noindent
(@code{List.init n f} builds the list @code{[f 0; f 1; @dots{}; f (n - 1)]};
here it stands in for the range @code{1} through @code{10}.)

@subsubheading Deriving @code{map} and @code{filter} by hand

Before reaching for the library, this section rebuilds both by hand, the way
the original book rebuilds list primitives from @code{cons}, @code{car}, and
@code{cdr} to show they hold no magic:

@example
# let rec my_map f = function
    | [] -> []
    | head :: tail -> f head :: my_map f tail;;
val my_map : ('a -> 'b) -> 'a list -> 'b list = <fun>
# my_map (fun x -> x * x) [ 1; 2; 3 ];;
- : int list = [1; 4; 9]
# let rec my_filter predicate = function
    | [] -> []
    | head :: tail ->
      if predicate head
      then head :: my_filter predicate tail
      else my_filter predicate tail;;
val my_filter : ('a -> bool) -> 'a list -> 'a list = <fun>
# my_filter (fun x -> x > 2) [ 1; 2; 3; 4 ];;
- : int list = [3; 4]
@end example

@noindent
Both are teaching forms, not the versions this book calls elsewhere:
@code{my_map} and @code{my_filter} recurse on the @code{::} pattern
directly, and neither is tail recursive, so both grow one pending
@code{::} per element on the call stack — fine for the short lists this
chapter uses, a real limit on a list
long enough to matter.

@subsubheading Folding, and why the library version is the everyday choice

A fold reduces a whole list to one accumulated value, generalizing both
@code{map} and @code{filter} (and @code{total} and @code{tree_sum} from the
previous section) into one shape: an accumulator, a combining function, and
a list to walk.

@example
# let my_fold_left f acc xs =
    let rec go acc = function
      | [] -> acc
      | head :: tail -> go (f acc head) tail
    in
    go acc xs;;
val my_fold_left : ('a -> 'b -> 'a) -> 'a -> 'b list -> 'a = <fun>
# List.fold_left ( + ) 0 [ 1; 2; 3; 4 ];;
- : int = 10
# my_fold_left ( + ) 0 [ 1; 2; 3; 4 ];;
- : int = 10
@end example

@noindent
Unlike @code{my_map} and @code{my_filter}, the inner @code{go} here is tail
recursive: each call to @code{go} either returns immediately or makes
exactly one more call to @code{go}, with nothing left pending on the stack
in between. That is the concrete reason @code{List.fold_left} (and, by
extension, this hand-rolled version) is the everyday choice once a list
might be long: it runs in constant stack space, where @code{my_map} and
@code{my_filter} do not. The library ships tail-recursive @code{map} and
@code{filter} too; the hand-rolled, non-tail-recursive versions above exist
only to show what the recursion looks like before that optimization is
applied.

@subsubheading Pipeline style

@code{|>} applies its left operand as the argument to the function on its
right, letting a chain of transformations read top to bottom in the order
they run, rather than inside-out:

@example
# [ 1; 2; 3; 4; 5 ]
  |> List.map (fun x -> x * x)
  |> List.filter (fun x -> x mod 2 = 1);;
- : int list = [1; 9; 25]
@end example

@noindent
This is purely a matter of reading order — @code{|>} is defined as
@code{let (|>) x f = f x}, nothing more — but a pipeline of several steps
reads far more like a description of a process (``take these, square them,
keep the odd ones'') than the equivalent nested calls would, and this book
uses the pipeline form wherever a value passes through more than one
transformation in sequence.
